# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  TRIAGED -- decided, with the reason the header asks for.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  HeapHolders is the WORKING of IsSole, not a second answer. IsSole asks "can a
#  write through this be seen anywhere but here", and answers it by comparing the
#  heap's reference count against the references this object and the sub-objects it
#  owns are holding; HeapHolders is that second number. The count on its own is only
#  meaningful next to P2PmsgHeap_RefCount, which the flat surface does not expose, and
#  it takes a P2PmsgHANDLE, which is not a type the flat surface has.
#
#  THIS REASON SAID ONE THING THAT WAS NOT TRUE, and section 34 is where that was
#  measured. It read "a caller on the flat surface wants the answer and has it --
#  IsSole is the question the C API would carry". The caller does not have it: there
#  is no msgcore_field_is_sole in Msgcore_c.h, and none of the nine
#  msgcore_field_is_* predicates answers this question. IsSole scored BOUND only
#  through check_api_drift.ps1's prefix fallback, which rooted 'is_sole' at 'is' and
#  matched msgcore_field_is_null. The argument above survives the correction whole --
#  the working of a question is not the question -- but whether the flat surface
#  should carry the question itself was left open rather than settled. Refer
#  stack_paths.md sections 29 and 34; the fallback no longer scores it, and IsSole
#  carries a line of its own at the bottom of this file.
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgField::HeapHolders    # the working of IsSole, and takes a P2PmsgHANDLE
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgDesc::HeapHolders    # as P3PmsgField::HeapHolders, one level in
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgAttr::HeapHolders    # as P3PmsgField::HeapHolders, one level in
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgCurs::HeapHolders    # as P3PmsgField::HeapHolders; P3PmsgCurs has no flat surface at all
cxx-to-c  MsgStck::HeapHolders    # as P3PmsgField::HeapHolders, one level in
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgList::HeapHolders    # as P3PmsgField::HeapHolders, plus the data cursors the list caches
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgVect::HeapHolders    # as P3PmsgField::HeapHolders, plus the element cursor the vect caches
#  P3PmsgData::HeapHolders, added by the same section, needs no line: P3PmsgData has
#  no family in api-drift.config.psd1 and is therefore never scanned. The config says
#  why in its own comment -- the class has no C projection by design.
#
#  Drop frees the item in the store; destroy releases a HANDLE to it. The flat
#  surface already spells the second, once per family -- msgcore_field_destroy,
#  msgcore_attr_destroy, msgcore_desc_destroy, msgcore_list_destroy,
#  msgcore_vect_destroy -- so an export named msgcore_vect_drop sitting beside
#  msgcore_vect_destroy would be two names that read the same and do the opposite.
#  The surface removes things the other way round: it asks the PARENT and names the
#  child (msgcore_field_delete_item, msgcore_attr_delete, msgcore_desc_delete,
#  msgcore_list_delete_at, msgcore_vect_delete), or it empties in place
#  (msgcore_*_truncate). Both spellings leave every handle the caller is holding
#  valid. Drop asked of the item ITSELF cannot: it frees the block other live
#  handles alias, and the durable re-resolution route rule 2 of Msgcore_c.h offers
#  against relocation -- hold the p2pos, come back through msgcore_mgr_p2pos2field
#  -- resolves to nothing once the item is gone. The flat ABI has no way to tell a
#  handle that it died, so it does not hand out the call that kills one.
#
#  Two notes, because "drop is internal" would not have been a reason here. First,
#  P3PmsgVect::Drop was written by d2763ce for exactly one caller -- MsgStck::Pop
#  freeing the generation it had just unlinked, "no vect had ever been a pushed
#  stack generation" (MsgVect.cpp:585) -- and that caller IS on the flat surface, as
#  msgcore_stck_pop, which reaches Drop from the inside. Second, msgcore_stck_drop
#  is not a counter-example: "Release the stacked position without restoring it"
#  (Msgcore_c.h:886) frees no item the caller holds a handle on and unlinks nothing
#  from the tree.
#
#  P3PmsgList::Drop (MsgList.h:122) is the same member under the same reason, and it
#  spent one section with no line here BECAUSE IT COULD NOT CARRY ONE: the prefix
#  fallback scored it BOUND against msgcore_list_drop_head -- the root was
#  'msgcore_list_drop' and msgcore_list_drop_head starts with it -- so a line would
#  have come back reported as redundant and the next reader told to delete it.
#  msgcore_list_drop_head and _drop_tail bind P3PmsgList::DropHead and ::DropTail,
#  which take one element off an end, and the tightened matcher now says so: a
#  function that is already another member's exact binding is not evidence for this
#  one. The line below is that reason promoted out of this comment, where section 34
#  parked it.
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgVect::Drop    # frees the item; every drop-shaped name on the flat surface releases a handle instead
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgList::Drop    # as P3PmsgVect::Drop; msgcore_list_drop_head and _drop_tail are DropHead's and DropTail's
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgField::Drop    # as P3PmsgVect::Drop; the surface deletes from the parent, by name
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgAttr::Drop    # as P3PmsgVect::Drop, of a collection; msgcore_attr_truncate empties one without freeing it
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgDesc::Drop    # as P3PmsgVect::Drop, of a collection; msgcore_desc_truncate empties one without freeing it
#
#  == and != on a field ask IDENTITY -- the same heap handle and the same block
#  address, and therefore the SAME ITEM -- and not value equality, which is r_data()
#  and r_name(). Refer stack_paths.md section 21. The flat surface already carries
#  that question, as a VALUE rather than as a predicate: msgcore_field_get_p2pos is
#  "the natural inode number (st_ino)" (Msgcore_c.h:547), and two handles denote the
#  same item exactly when their p2pos are equal and non-zero, compared with the
#  caller's own ==. It is the STRONGER of the two tests, which is why exposing the
#  operator would make the surface worse rather than wider: the operator compares a
#  block ADDRESS, and rule 2 at the top of Msgcore_c.h says any allocating call may
#  move every block, so a msgcore_field_equals would be answerable only between two
#  handles re-resolved since the last mutation, where the p2pos comparison survives
#  one. The residue is small and worth naming: p2pos returns 0 for an unaddressable
#  standalone field, so two distinct FLOATING fields compare equal on the flat
#  surface where the operator would separate them.
#
#  The name comparison this same name also carries, operator == ( LPCTNAM ), is
#  answered by msgcore_field_get_name plus the caller's own compare, and by
#  msgcore_field_exists for the lookup form. Neither half of the name is missing
#  from the surface; both are spelled as something other than an operator.
#
#  != is here and == was UNTRIAGED for no reason but timing. 4f4c334 added both
#  overloads on the same day. == was already a banked NAME, because
#  operator == ( LPCTNAM ) predates it (P2Pmsg.h:720 at 4f4c334^), and the check
#  asks about each distinct name once, so the new == collapsed into the line that
#  already existed and never resurfaced; != was a new name and surfaced at once.
#  They are triaged together because they are one decision.
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgField::operator!=    # identity, which msgcore_field_get_p2pos already carries as a comparable value
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgField::operator==    # as operator!=; and the LPCTNAM overload is msgcore_field_get_name
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  The thirty-five the tightened matcher surfaced.
#
#  check_api_drift.ps1's prefix fallback used to root a C++ member at its first
#  snake-cased segment and take whatever the surface listed first, which scored
#  GetHeadPos against msgcore_list_get_count and IsSole against
#  msgcore_field_is_null. Section 34 measured that -- 43 of 116 "bound" came
#  through the fallback and 28 of those rooted at a bare accessor verb -- and
#  deliberately left it, because tightening it turns silent passes into questions
#  about the C API and that is a session of its own. This is that session. The
#  honest bound count is 81, not 116, and the thirty-five members below are the
#  difference. None of them was decided before today; every one of them was
#  reported green.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#  A LIST POSITION IS A RAW ARENA OFFSET, AND THE ABI CANNOT HAND ONE OUT.
#  GetHeadPos and GetTailPos return VBLaddr, and VBLaddr is `#define VBLaddr
#  UINT_PTR` (Msgcore.h:49) -- an offset into the heap image, pointer-wide, which
#  Msgcore.h:71 says to "key off pointer width on every toolchain". Two things
#  follow and either alone is enough. It is not a stable VALUE: rule 2 at the top
#  of Msgcore_c.h says any allocating call may move every block, and a position
#  held across one addresses whatever now occupies that offset -- the same defect
#  as a stale p2pos, without p2pos's re-resolution route through
#  msgcore_mgr_p2pos2field. And it is not a stable TYPE: an export returning
#  UINT_PTR makes the flat ABI pointer-width-dependent, which is the x86/x64 split
#  section 33 spent a section on.
#
#  So the surface walks a list the two other ways, and both are re-validated on
#  every call. By ordinal -- msgcore_list_get_int_at, _get_wstr_at, _set_int_at,
#  _delete_at, all bounded by msgcore_list_get_count -- and by cursor, the whole
#  msgcore_curs_ family, which owns the position rather than handing it to the
#  caller. GetNext, GetPrev and GetTail are the same walk from the other end: they
#  return P3PmsgData&, a reference into the live object, and P3PmsgData has no
#  family in api-drift.config.psd1 at all because it has no C projection by design.
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgList::GetHeadPos    # returns VBLaddr, a pointer-wide arena offset rule 2 invalidates; the surface indexes by ordinal
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgList::GetTailPos    # as GetHeadPos, from the other end
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgList::GetNext    # the VBLaddr walk, and it returns P3PmsgData&, which the surface does not carry
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgList::GetPrev    # as GetNext, backwards
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgList::GetTail    # returns P3PmsgData&; the surface reads element get_count-1 by ordinal
#
#  BLOCK GEOMETRY IS THE ARENA'S BUSINESS AND MFC'S POSITION IS NOT A C TYPE.
#  GetVBLockType takes a POSITION (MsgList.h:125, MsgVect.h:101), which is MFC's
#  list cookie and exactly the case this file's own header names -- "takes a
#  P2PmsgHANDLE, which is not a type the flat surface has" -- and it answers with
#  the storage block's type code, the uVBLockDefs nibble section 36 documents for
#  the image scanner. GetVBLockListSize and GetVBLockVectSize answer with the
#  block's byte size.
#
#  The surface asks the neighbouring question and asks it about CONTENT rather
#  than storage: msgcore_list_get_type_at and msgcore_vect_get_type report an
#  element's data type, msgcore_field_get_data_type a field's, and
#  msgcore_type_name / msgcore_type_from_name give that vocabulary names. A block
#  type code has no such vocabulary on the flat side, and publishing one would put
#  the arena's header layout into the supported ABI, where every future change to
#  the format becomes a breaking change.
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgList::GetVBLockType    # takes an MFC POSITION and answers with a block type code; the surface reports data types
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgVect::GetVBLockType    # as P3PmsgList::GetVBLockType
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgList::GetVBLockListSize    # the storage block's byte size; arena layout, not message content
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgVect::GetVBLockVectSize    # as GetVBLockListSize
#
#  THE LIBRARY'S OWN HANDLES ARE NOT THE SURFACE'S HANDLES. P2PmsgFieldHdl is
#  three bare UINT_PTRs (P2PmsgVBLock.h:276-281) and its list and vect twins are
#  the same shape; P2PmsgHANDLE is the heap handle the HeapHolders block above
#  already refuses for being no type of the surface's. MsgFieldHandle and its
#  siblings are opaque by construction: a caller obtains one from
#  msgcore_mgr_root, _p2pos2field, _field_child or _from_field, and returns it
#  through the matching _destroy. Exporting a getter for the internal struct would
#  publish a pointer-width record of heap handle and block address -- unowned,
#  undestroyable, and invalid after the next allocation -- beside the opaque handle
#  the rules are written for. There would then be two handle vocabularies in one
#  ABI and only one set of rules.
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgField::GetP2PmsgFieldHdl    # returns three raw UINT_PTRs; the surface's handles are opaque and caller-owned
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgList::GetP2PmsgListHdl    # as GetP2PmsgFieldHdl
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgVect::GetP2PmsgVectHdl    # as GetP2PmsgFieldHdl
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgField::GetP2PmsgHandle    # returns P2PmsgHANDLE, the heap handle, which is not a type the flat surface has
#
#  AN EXPORT THAT RETURNS BITS NOBODY CAN NAME CANNOT BE USED. GetAccess masks
#  VBLockField::uAccessAttr (P2Pmsg.cpp:4279-4283) and GetPermissions masks
#  VBLockDesc::uPermissions and VBLockAttr::uPermissions (MsgDesc.cpp:773-779).
#  The access byte at least has a vocabulary -- AttrField_HIDDEN, _NOCOPY, _SORT,
#  _TOUCH, _EXPAND (P2PmsgVBLock.h:283-289) -- but it is declared in a header
#  Msgcore_c.h does not include and a C caller does not have, so a
#  msgcore_field_get_access would answer with a UCHAR whose bits the caller cannot
#  spell. The permission byte is worse: grep the repository and nothing names a
#  single bit of it anywhere.  Exporting either means exporting the constants too,
#  and the constants are the arena's on-disk semantics, which is the commitment
#  the block-geometry reason above declines for the same reason.
#
#  SetAccess is here and its two SetPermissions counterparts are still UNTRIAGED
#  above. That is not a distinction: SetAccess surfaced with this batch and they
#  did not, and moving lines nobody asked about would move the UNTRIAGED count out
#  from under the sections that quote it. They are the same argument when somebody
#  reaches them.
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgField::GetAccess    # masks a block-header byte whose bit names live in a header the C ABI does not publish
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgField::SetAccess    # as GetAccess, writing; the same unspellable bits
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgDesc::GetPermissions    # masks uPermissions, a byte no header in this repository names a single bit of
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgAttr::GetPermissions    # as P3PmsgDesc::GetPermissions
#
#  THE DIRTY FLAG BELONGS TO THE C++ OBJECT, NOT TO THE ITEM. P3PmsgField::IsDirty
#  is m_bFieldDirty || P3PmsgData::IsDirty() || P3PmsgName::IsDirty()
#  (P2Pmsg.cpp:4293-4300) and the vect's adds m_bVectDirty (MsgVect.cpp:1111-1117).
#  Every one of those is an in-memory flag on the wrapper, set by writes made
#  THROUGH THAT WRAPPER, and nothing in the block records it. Two flat handles onto
#  the same item are two C++ objects with two flags, so a msgcore_field_is_dirty
#  would answer "has anything been written through this particular handle since it
#  was made" -- a question about the handle's own short life, which rule 2 already
#  tells callers to keep short.
#
#  The question that survives handles being minted and destroyed is the store's,
#  and the surface carries it: msgcore_mgr_is_dirty and msgcore_mgr_set_dirty,
#  which is what a caller deciding whether to msgcore_mgr_save actually needs.
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgField::IsDirty    # a flag on the C++ wrapper, not on the item; msgcore_mgr_is_dirty outlives a handle
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgVect::IsDirty    # as P3PmsgField::IsDirty, plus m_bVectDirty
#
#  THE PARENT POINTER IS SOMETHING THE FLAT CALLER ALREADY HAS. GetField returns
#  P3PmsgField* -- the owning field of the collection (MsgDesc.h:211, MsgAttr.h:182).
#  On the flat surface a collection handle can only have come from a field handle,
#  through msgcore_desc_from_field or msgcore_attr_from_field, so the caller is
#  holding the answer before it asks. Handing it back would also break the one rule
#  the handle group states without exception -- "Returned handles are caller-owned
#  (msgcore_field_destroy)" (Msgcore_c.h:523-524) -- because this one is not: it
#  aliases the collection's owner, and destroying it would take down the tree the
#  collection is standing in.
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgDesc::GetField    # the owning field, which a flat caller already holds: msgcore_desc_from_field is the only way in
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgAttr::GetField    # as P3PmsgDesc::GetField
#
#  SELECT BY VALUE AND SELECT BY REFERENCE ARE ONE CALL IN C. Select returns
#  P3PmsgField by VALUE and SelectItem returns P3PmsgField& (MsgDesc.h:158-165,
#  MsgAttr.h:131-138); the pair exists because C++ callers choose between a
#  detached copy and an alias into the collection. The flat surface has no such
#  choice to offer: msgcore_desc_select_item and msgcore_attr_select_item return an
#  opaque handle the caller owns and destroys, which is already the by-value form,
#  and rule 2 governs it either way. A second export would differ from the first in
#  nothing a C caller could observe.
#
#  SelectObject is refused for a different reason and a harder one: it returns
#  P3PmsgObject, and P3PmsgObject is one of the four classes api-drift.config.psd1
#  keeps out of TypeMap on purpose, because it has no C projection by design. The
#  surface answers "what kind of thing is this" the way section 20 settled it --
#  msgcore_field_is_list, _is_vect, _is_attr, _is_desc, _is_null, _is_void asked of
#  a field handle -- rather than by handing over an untyped node.
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgDesc::Select    # by-value select; msgcore_desc_select_item already returns an owned handle, which is the same thing in C
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgAttr::Select    # as P3PmsgDesc::Select
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgDesc::SelectObject    # returns P3PmsgObject, a class the config keeps off the surface by design
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgAttr::SelectObject    # as P3PmsgDesc::SelectObject
#
#  IsName IS DECLARED AND NEVER DEFINED. MsgVect.h:145 declares it beside IsData,
#  IsField, IsList and IsVect, and MsgVect.cpp defines those four and not this one
#  -- the whole repository contains no P3PmsgVect::IsName body. Its four siblings
#  bind exactly, to msgcore_vect_is_data, _is_field, _is_list and _is_vect. This
#  one cannot be bound because there is nothing to bind to: a wrapper calling it
#  would not link. That is a defect in MsgVect.cpp rather than a decision about the
#  C API, and it is recorded here as the former.
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgVect::IsName    # declared at MsgVect.h:145 and defined nowhere; a binding would not link
#
#  A PATH IS A MANAGER'S QUESTION. GetPath returns a CString built by walking from
#  this field up to the root, and CString is an MFC type the flat surface does not
#  have. The surface asks the same question of the manager and answers in wchar_t*:
#  msgcore_mgr_p2pos2path, reached with msgcore_field_get_p2pos -- two calls that a
#  field-side export would collapse into one only by giving every field handle a
#  manager it does not carry.
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgField::GetPath    # returns CString; msgcore_field_get_p2pos + msgcore_mgr_p2pos2path is the flat spelling
#
#  GetP2Pos IS BOUND AND THE MATCHER CANNOT SEE IT. msgcore_field_get_p2pos is the
#  binding, quoted by rule 2 itself at Msgcore_c.h:108-109 and again at :531 as the
#  durable alternative to a live handle. It does not match because ConvertTo-Snake
#  splits GetP2Pos into get_p2_pos while the surface spells get_p2pos, and no
#  sequence of general rules turns one into the other without inventing a
#  dictionary. This is the extractor, not the design -- the third of the three ways
#  out the check prints -- and the line exists so that the next reader is not told a
#  bound member is unbound.
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgField::GetP2Pos    # bound as msgcore_field_get_p2pos; the snake-caser spells it get_p2_pos and cannot match
#
#  PageRegistration IS BOUND UNDER A DIFFERENT NAME, TWICE. The two overloads
#  (P2PmsgMgr.h:211-215) install the page-in/page-out pair and the populate
#  callback, and the surface spells them msgcore_mgr_set_paging_sinks and
#  msgcore_mgr_set_populate_sink (Msgcore_c.h:1027-1032), with msgcore_pagein_fn,
#  msgcore_pageout_fn and msgcore_populate_fn as the C signatures of the three
#  P2Page*CBFnc types. The rename is deliberate and documented -- the C header
#  spends fifteen lines on how a sink must behave and how it differs from the
#  trigger sink -- so the missing binding is the matcher's, not the surface's.
#  PageRegistrationPush and PageRegistrationPop bind exactly and are a different
#  pair of calls.
cxx-to-c  P2PmsgMgr::PageRegistration    # bound as msgcore_mgr_set_paging_sinks and msgcore_mgr_set_populate_sink; a rename the matcher cannot follow
#
#  THE PATH GRAMMAR HAS NO FLAT SPELLING, AND THAT IS THIS INVESTIGATION'S OWN
#  SUBJECT. RootPath2Object takes an absolute object path and resolves it, which
#  means it implements the grammar sections 2 to 5 and 17 to 19 are about: the
#  leading '.' root marker, '.' descent, '@' into attributes, and '^' into the
#  pushed generation. The flat surface carries no path parser at all. It descends
#  one named step at a time -- msgcore_mgr_root, then msgcore_field_child or
#  msgcore_field_select_item -- and it has no spelling whatever for '@' or '^',
#  which are the two components that make the grammar more than string
#  concatenation.
#
#  Exporting a path resolver would therefore not be adding a convenience; it would
#  commit the supported ABI to a grammar whose corner cases this document needed
#  thirty-odd sections to pin down, and every one of those cases would become an
#  ABI promise. msgcore_mgr_p2pos2path goes the other way -- position to text --
#  and commits to nothing, which is why it is there and this is not.
cxx-to-c  P2PmsgMgr::RootPath2Object    # resolves the '.'/'@'/'^' path grammar; the surface descends a step at a time and spells neither '@' nor '^'
#
#  A MANAGER-SIDE PREDICATE KEYED ON A POSITION IS TWO CALLS ON THE FLAT SIDE.
#  IsField ( P2Pos ) and IsAttributed ( UCHAR, P2Pos ) ask about the object at a
#  position rather than about the manager, so the flat caller resolves first --
#  msgcore_mgr_p2pos2field -- and then asks the field: msgcore_field_is_list and
#  msgcore_field_is_vect settle IsField between them, and
#  msgcore_field_is_attributed answers the unmasked half of IsAttributed. The
#  masked half is the access byte again, and the reason it is not exported is the
#  one two blocks up: the bits have no names on this side of the boundary.
#
#  Two calls instead of one is the shape the whole handle group is written in --
#  obtain, use, discard -- and a manager-side shortcut would have to hold a
#  resolved field across the caller's next mutation, which rule 2 forbids.
cxx-to-c  P2PmsgMgr::IsField    # p2pos2field then msgcore_field_is_list / _is_vect; the surface asks the item, not the manager
cxx-to-c  P2PmsgMgr::IsAttributed    # as IsField; msgcore_field_is_attributed answers the unmasked half and the mask is the unnamed access byte
#
#  IsSole AND IsInline: NOT CARRIED, AND THE DECISION IS THE OWNER'S TO REVISIT.
#  These are the two members section 34 named. IsSole asks whether a write through
#  this field can be seen anywhere but here and its TRUE is a guarantee (section 26,
#  narrowed by sections 29 and 31); IsInline asks whether the block lives inside the
#  field object, where its TRUE is a guarantee and its FALSE is not (section 24).
#  Both scored BOUND against msgcore_field_is_null through the old 'is' root, which
#  is how section 29's HeapHolders reason came to say a flat caller "wants the
#  answer and has it". It does not have it. There is no msgcore_field_is_sole and no
#  msgcore_field_is_inline, and none of the nine msgcore_field_is_* predicates
#  answers either question.
#
#  The case FOR carrying IsSole is real and should be written down rather than
#  argued away. msgcore_field_is_sole ( MsgFieldHandle ) would be
#  signature-identical to the nine predicates already there, it needs no type the
#  surface lacks, and its TRUE is the one fact a flat caller cannot reconstruct from
#  anything else on the surface: p2pos answers identity, not exclusivity, and there
#  is no flat route to P2PmsgHeap_RefCount. A TreeFS host deciding whether it may
#  write in place has exactly this question.
#
#  The case AGAINST is that it is an ADDITION TO THE SUPPORTED ABI. Msgcore_c.h is
#  the 282-function surface the README calls supported; a new export bumps
#  Msgcore_version.h in the same commit, and that is a versioning decision belonging
#  to whoever owns the release, not to a check that reads names. There is a second
#  half: IsSole's FALSE still promises nothing (section 26's asymmetry, unmoved by
#  sections 29 and 31, and the per-block reference count is still a different
#  library), so the export would be a predicate that is load-bearing in one
#  direction and advisory in the other, and the header would have to say so in as
#  many words. IsInline is the same shape and weaker: its FALSE is explicitly not a
#  guarantee (section 24), and it describes where storage sits rather than what the
#  caller may do, which is the block-geometry line this file has already drawn twice.
#
#  So: not carried, today, by this file. The lines below record that, not a refusal
#  in principle. Adding msgcore_field_is_sole remains a live option and the argument
#  for it is above, in full, on purpose.
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgField::IsSole    # no msgcore_field_is_sole exists; carrying one is an ABI addition and a version bump, and is the owner's call
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgField::IsInline    # as IsSole, and weaker: its FALSE guarantees nothing and it describes storage placement
